<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nizam of Hyderabad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nizam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> was the title of the rulers of Hyderabad State in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, a dynasty founded by a former Mughal governor in the early eighteenth century. The Nizams were major rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, which fought them repeatedly and imposed tribute arrangements. Hyderabad later became the largest princely state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad.jpg" alt="Nizam of Hyderabad" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nizam of Hyderabad, India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hyderabad_Coat_of_Arms.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Nizam is a shortened form of Niẓām ul-Mulk, Administrator of the Realm, the title given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a> when the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukhsiyar">Farrukhsiyar</a> appointed him viceroy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Asaf Jah I, born Mir Qamaruddin Khan, descended from two prominent Mughal families. His grandfather Abid Khan, a Persian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, became a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, and his father Ghazi Uddin Khan helped conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> sultanates in 1686. His mother was a daughter of the former grand vizier Sa'dullah Khan. The Nizams ruled Hyderabad State, now parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, the Marathwada region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and the Kalyana-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> region of Karnataka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1724 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubariz-khan">Mubariz Khan</a> to establish autonomy over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jahi-dynasty">Asaf Jahi dynasty</a>. He never formally declared independence: he flew the Mughal flag, was never crowned, and Friday sermons were given in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, a practice that lasted until 1948. After his death in 1748 his sons, backed by neighbouring states and foreign forces, contended for the throne, and the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-ali-khan">Asaf Jah II</a>, who reigned from 1762 to 1803, ended the instability. In 1768 he signed the treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machilipatnam">Machilipatnam</a>, surrendering the coastal region to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> for a fixed annual rent.</p><p>From the 1720s the Nizams fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at Palkhed, Bhopal, Rakshasbhuvan and Kharda, losing each time, and paid them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauth">chauth</a>, a regular tax. In 1805, after the Company's victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-maratha-war">Second Anglo-Maratha War</a>, the Nizam came under British protection. The title His Exalted Highness and Faithful Ally of the British Government rewarded the Nizams' role in the Second and Third Anglo-Mysore Wars and the rebellion of 1857. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> region was separated in 1903.</p><p>The seventh Nizam, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-osman-ali-khan">Mir Osman Ali Khan</a>, was crowned in 1911. During his reign the state gained railways, electricity, roads, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osmania-university">Osmania University</a>, and 80,000 men raised by him served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In 1947 he chose to join neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and sought to be an independent monarchy within the Commonwealth, which India and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a> rejected. The Razakars, who wanted accession to Pakistan, are described as committing atrocities against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> invaded on 13 September 1948, an operation in which massacres of Muslims are also recorded. The Nizam capitulated on 17 September 1948.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The dynasty had only seven recognised rulers. The Nizams founded hospitals, schools and colleges teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, a Hyderabad Civil Service, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-sagar">Osman Sagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himayat-sagar">Himayat Sagar</a>. Mir Osman Ali Khan became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a> after accession and died on 24 February 1967. The seventh Nizam also gave grants to temples, churches and gurdwaras. The title is now held by his great-grandson, a pretender.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nizam of Hyderabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mysore also fought the Marathas and the <b>Nizam of Hyderabad</b>, who became allies of the British in the Third and Fourth Anglo-Mysore Wars that destroyed the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Maratha ascendancy redrew the political map, compelling alliances, tribute arrangements and settlements with the <b>Nizam of Hyderabad</b>, the Rajput states, the Nawabs of Bengal and the Sikh chiefs, and under Mahadji Scindia the Marathas controlled Delhi and the Mughal emperor in the 1770s and 1780s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 1920s the last <b>Nizam of Hyderabad</b>, whose territory included the site, had the paintings restored by two Italian experts and opened a museum and a road, efforts that Cohen says were mismanaged and hastened decay; according to Spink and others, Griffiths's cheap varnish and recent…</blockquote><a href="/work/ajanta-caves">Ajanta Caves · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maratha Empire defeated the combined army of the <b>Nizam of Hyderabad</b> and several Mughal generals at Bhopal, a coalition that also took in the Rajputs of Jaipur and the Nawabs of Awadh and Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Berar later passed to the Mughal Empire, then to the <b>Nizams of Hyderabad</b> and finally the British.</blockquote><a href="/state/berar-sultanate">Berar Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1692 Aurangzeb appointed Zulfiqar Ali Khan as Nawab of the Carnatic, and the governorship, based at Arcot, became hereditary under Saadatullah Khan, nominally subordinate to the <b>Nizam of Hyderabad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Carnatic Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
